// Practice quiz: Mockito in Spring: @MockitoBean
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing/mockito-in-spring-at-mockitobean

::o1 what it does::What does @MockitoBean do in a Spring test? {
~Starts a real server#Not quite. That is not its purpose.
~Deletes a repository#Not quite. It only affects the test context.
=Registers a Mockito mock as a bean in the context#Correct. Other beans receive the mock.
~Writes test data to the database#Not quite. It does not touch data.
}

::o1 unstubbed default::A mock method returning a list is not stubbed. What does it return when called? {
~It throws#Not quite. By default, it does not throw.
=An empty list#Correct. Mockito returns simple defaults.
~The real result#Not quite. A mock does not call real code.
~Random data#Not quite. Results are deterministic.
}

::o2 verifying::How do you check that a mock method was never called? {
~when(mock).never()#Not quite. That is not the API.
~assertNull(mock)#Not quite. That checks the reference.
~Remove the method#Not quite. That is not a test.
=verify(mock, never()).method(...)#Correct. never() asserts zero calls.
}

::o2 plain mock::How does a plain Mockito mock differ from @MockitoBean? {
=Spring does not know about it, so nothing in the context receives it#Correct. It lives only in the test.
~It cannot be stubbed#Not quite. It can be stubbed the same way.
~It runs real code#Not quite. Mocks do not run real code.
~It requires H2#Not quite. It does not.
}

::o3 choosing::You need to test a service rule that uses one repository interface. What is the smallest fitting test? {
~A full @SpringBootTest#Not quite. That is much heavier than needed.
=A plain unit test with a fake#Correct. No Spring behavior is under test.
~A browser test#Not quite. Nothing here involves a browser.
~No test, since it is simple#Not quite. Rules deserve tests.
}

